- op: discuss  # 5f24
  at: '2026-07-31T00:56:48Z'  # 5f24
  by: agent  # 5f24
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5f24
  clock: 7  # 5f24
  nonce: 5f24  # 5f24
  message: "Fixed in PR #103, merged to dev a0028ae050. Owner approved the governance change (scripts/auto-pr) in-session, without --gov.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE, verified on merged dev rather than inferred from the merge:\n\n1. Live repro of the damage, from scratch, no tracker involved -- init a repo, commit \"feat: a real base commit\", checkout -b, stage work without committing, amend: the work lands wearing the base commit's subject. This is the positive control and it was the ONLY test green before the fix, which is what establishes the guard tests measure something git actually does rather than a scenario that cannot occur.\n\n2. Guard predicate, both arms measured -- branch with no commits of its own: merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD dev TRUE, refuses. Branch with its own commit, including after a rebase onto an advanced base: FALSE, silent. So it catches the failure and does not block the normal flow.\n\n3. Production path exercised during this very PR -- auto-pr's own run printed \"Manifest updated -- amending commit\", reaching the guarded site at :1430 with the guard live. It passed correctly because the branch owned its HEAD. The fix was exercised by the mechanism it fixes.\n\n4. On merged dev: all three amend sites (auto-pr:1074, :1476, :1516) each preceded by a guard call (:1073, :1474, :1515); tests/test_auto_pr.py 6 passed.\n\nA ratchet test locates every `git commit --amend` in the script and fails if one is unguarded, so a newly-added amend cannot regress this silently.\n\nSCOPE ACTUALLY DELIVERED vs FILED. The three candidate fixes in the original description were all wrong and none were implemented: (a) no-op (the tracker has zero --amend calls), (b) orthogonal (the sync's fast-forward preceded branch creation by 16s), (c) already the behavior (DESC read at :1428, amend at :1430). What shipped is the ownership guard, which is a different fix than any of the three.\n\nSTILL OPEN, deliberately: (b) auto-sync deferring while .git/PR_PENDING exists. It is a real hazard AGENTS.md documents, it just is not this bug. Not filed as a sub-item; raise it if you want it.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and it does not change the fix: whether the 2026-07-29 amend was issued by auto-pr's manifest step or typed by hand. The reflog cannot distinguish them. The guard closes the hole either way -- which is the argument for the structural fix over a procedural rule.\n\nSPUN OUT: WI-bisar. Naming the new suite tests/test_auto_pr.py was load-bearing, not cosmetic -- under its original descriptive name smart-test selected 0 tests and ci.yml:811 skipped pytest, so the ratchet would have passed locally and never executed in CI. That exposed a larger gap: the WI-jozan mapper is 1:1, so the ten pre-existing tests/test_autopr_*.py files (141 tests) are selected by nothing when only scripts/auto-pr changes."  # 5f24
- op: discuss  # b1bb
  at: '2026-07-31T02:01:21Z'  # b1bb
  by: agent  # b1bb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b1bb
  clock: 8  # b1bb
  nonce: b1bb  # b1bb
  message: "Fix (b) checked before implementing. It is ALREADY IMPLEMENTED, in two layers, and predates this item's filing by five months. Nothing was built.\n\n  sync.py preflight_check, check #2 (hard refusal)\n      pr_pending = git_dir / \"PR_PENDING\"\n      if pr_pending.exists():\n          return PreflightResult(ok=False,\n              error=\"auto-pr in flight (PR_PENDING exists). Wait for it to complete.\")\n      landed 0af0b245af, 2026-02-18 -- this item was filed 2026-07-29.\n\n  cli.py _maybe_auto_sync (bounded wait, then skip)\n      waits up to _SYNC_PR_WAIT_TIMEOUT = 900s, polling every 10s, and\n      skips the sync if PR_PENDING has not cleared.\n\nBehaviorally verified on merged dev rather than read off the source:\n      preflight_check(repo) without PR_PENDING -> ok=True,  error=''\n      preflight_check(repo) with    PR_PENDING -> ok=False, error='auto-pr in flight (PR_PENDING exists). Wait for it to complete.'\n(the simulated gate file was removed afterwards and its absence re-asserted).\n\nThe mutual exclusion is symmetric already: auto-pr checks TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING via _autopr_sync_gate_held at three call sites (auto-pr:1118, :1391, :1594), and sync checks PR_PENDING as above. sync.py's module docstring documents the pairing explicitly.\n\nIt is also tested: packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests/test_cli.py:5085 is a dedicated class covering all three arms (waits until cleared, times out and skips, proceeds when absent), and test_sync.py:1538 covers the preflight refusal.\n\nThe stale-gate failure mode I wanted to rule out is handled. PR_PENDING is a plain file with no flock, so a SIGKILLed auto-pr leaks it -- but the wait is BOUNDED and degrades to a skipped sync rather than blocking auto-sync forever. (The other gate, TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING, is flock-held, which the OS releases even on SIGKILL, so it cannot go stale at all.)\n\nNET RESULT FOR THIS ITEM: all three filed candidate fixes are now disproven.\n  (a) \"auto-sync must never --amend\"        -> no-op; the tracker has zero --amend calls.\n  (b) \"auto-sync should defer on PR_PENDING\" -> already implemented since 2026-02-18, and tested.\n  (c) \"capture the body before auto-pr begins\" -> already the behavior; DESC is read at auto-pr:1428, the amend is at :1430.\nThe fix that actually shipped -- the amend-ownership guard -- was not among them. Worth noting as a pattern: three-for-three, the remedies proposed at filing time described a system that had already moved."  # b1bb
